---
// Research section. Accessible to a smart non-scientist, respectable to a
// specialist. No proprietary platform names, acronyms, or invented metrics.
---

<section class="section" id="research">
  <div class="wrap reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Research</p>
    <h2>Growing the human brain in a dish, to understand it and to heal it.</h2>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead">
        Brain organoids are small, self-organizing, three-dimensional models of human brain
        tissue, grown from stem cells. Assembloids join organoids together so that different
        brain regions can meet and form the connections they would in a living brain.
      </p>
      <p>
        These models let her study the developing human brain directly, rather than relying on
        animals alone. She uses them to ask what happens when development is disrupted, and to
        test whether a candidate therapy can put it back on course. The aim is to learn faster,
        and more humanely, about disorders that begin before or around birth.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="cards">
      <article class="card reveal">
        <h3>Injury to the newborn brain</h3>
        <p>
          When a baby is born early or deprived of oxygen, the brain can be injured in ways that
          lead to lifelong conditions such as cerebral palsy. She models these insults in human
          tissue to understand the damage and to search for ways to protect and repair it.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="card reveal">
        <h3>22q11.2 deletion syndrome</h3>
        <p>
          A small missing piece of one chromosome carries an outsized risk for the developing
          brain. She studies how it disrupts the cells that keep brain circuits in balance, and
          what might be done to restore them.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="card reveal">
        <h3>Epilepsy and circuit balance</h3>
        <p>
          Healthy brains hold a careful balance between signals that excite and signals that
          calm. When that balance breaks, seizures can follow. Her models help reveal how the
          balance is set during development, and how it fails.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="card reveal">
        <h3>From model to medicine</h3>
        <p>
          Human models are only worthwhile if they point to treatments. She works to carry
          findings from the dish toward real therapies, building the screening and testing steps
          that a candidate must pass on its way to patients.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="research-note">
      Why it matters: the disorders she works on touch some of the most vulnerable patients
      there are, often with few options today. Better human models are a faster, more honest
      path to the treatments those patients need.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .cards {
    margin-top: var(--space-l);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--line);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .cards {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }
  }

  .card {
    background: var(--paper);
    padding: clamp(1.3rem, 3vw, 1.9rem);
  }

  .card h3 {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .card p {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .research-note {
    margin-top: var(--space-l);
    max-width: var(--measure);
    padding-left: var(--space-m);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
</style>
